use amnimap
into raster.amni
lang amni

Module header: the lines "import math;", "import style;" and "import mvt;". No namespace. The Helium has no blank lines and no comments anywhere. This module draws vector geometry into RGBA pixel buffers with exact-area anti-aliasing, the way font-rs (Raph Levien's accumulation rasterizer) does, and renders whole map tiles with the style rules. Speed matters: it runs in WebAssembly on the page's main thread, so the inner loops work on f32 arrays by index, allocate nothing, and skip pixels that nothing touched.

```amni
struct Acc { w int = 0, h int = 0, stride int = 0, words int = 0, cov f32[>..] = [], mask u64[>..] = [], y0 int = 0, y1 int = 0 }
struct Xf { ox f32, oy f32, sc f32, bx f32, by f32 }
var acc_main Acc = Acc {};
```

An Acc is a coverage accumulator for an image w pixels wide and h high. Its `cov` holds stride (w plus 2) f32 cells per row, all 0 between draws. Its `mask` holds `words` u64 values per row, one bit per block of 32 cells (bit k of word j covers cells 32 times (64 j plus k) onward); a set bit means some cell in that block may be non-zero. y0 and y1 are the first row and one past the last row touched since the last composite (y0 is h and y1 is 0 when nothing is touched). An Xf maps tile units to pixels: pixel x is (x minus ox) times sc plus bx, and likewise y with oy and by.

`acc_init(a, w, h)` makes a ready for a w by h image: it sets w, h, stride (w plus 2) and words (enough u64 values per row for one bit per 32 cells of the stride), resizes cov to stride times h zeros and mask to words times h zeros (reusing the lists' memory; but when stride times h is more than cov's current length, cov and mask are each first replaced by a new empty list, because a growable list can never grow past the memory region it got when it first grew), and resets y0 to h and y1 to 0.

`acc_cell(a, row, col, v)` adds v to the cell at row and col (col is 0 or more and below stride) and sets that cell's block bit in the row's mask.

`acc_line(a, x0, y0, x1, y1)` adds the signed area coverage of one directed edge, exactly like font-rs's draw_line, with these steps. Return when the two y values are equal. Let dir be 1 when y0 is below y1, else swap the end points and let dir be -1, so y0 is now the smaller. Return when y1 is 0 or less or y0 is h or more. Split the edge where it crosses x equal 0 and x equal w (at most three pieces, in order), and handle each piece separately with the same dir after clamping both of its x values to between 0 and w, so geometry left of the image adds its winding at column 0 and geometry right of it lands in the spare columns. For a piece from (px0, py0) to (px1, py1) with py0 below py1: dxdy is (px1 minus px0) divided by (py1 minus py0); x starts at px0, moved by minus py0 times dxdy when py0 is below 0, then clamped to between 0 and w; rows run from the floor of py0 (at least 0) up to but not including the smaller of h and the ceiling of py1. For each row y: dy is the smaller of y plus 1 and py1, minus the larger of y and py0; xnext is x plus dxdy times dy, clamped to between 0 and w (adding dxdy row after row drifts a hair past the ends, which must never reach a column below 0); d is dy times dir; xa and xb are the smaller and larger of x and xnext; xa0 is the floor of xa as an int and xb1 the ceiling of xb as an int. When xb1 is at most xa0 plus 1: xmf is half of (x plus xnext) minus xa0, the cell at xa0 gets d minus d times xmf and the cell at xa0 plus 1 gets d times xmf. Otherwise: s is 1 divided by (xb minus xa); xaf is xa minus xa0; a0 is half of s times (1 minus xaf) squared; xbf is xb minus xb1 plus 1; am is half of s times xbf squared; the cell at xa0 gets d times a0; when xb1 equals xa0 plus 2 the cell at xa0 plus 1 gets d times (1 minus a0 minus am); otherwise a1 is s times (1.5 minus xaf), the cell at xa0 plus 1 gets d times (a1 minus a0), every cell from xa0 plus 2 up to but not including xb1 minus 1 gets d times s, a2 is a1 plus (xb1 minus xa0 minus 3) times s, and the cell at xb1 minus 1 gets d times (1 minus a2 minus am); then the cell at xb1 gets d times am. Then x becomes xnext. All adds go through `acc_cell`, and y0 and y1 of the Acc widen to include every row touched.

`acc_poly(a, xs, n)` adds a closed polygon whose n points are pairs in the f32 list xs (x then y, already in pixels): one `acc_line` per edge including the edge from the last point back to the first.

`acc_circle(a, cx, cy, r)` adds a closed regular polygon around (cx, cy) with radius r and 8 sides when r is below 3, 12 when below 8, else 20, walking the angle downward from 0 (angle k is minus 2 pi k divided by the side count) so it winds the same way as the stroke quads of `stroke_ring`. It never calls sin or cos per circle: the cosine and sine of every corner angle for 8, 12 and 20 sides are computed once into module f32 lists the first time they are needed, and the corners are written into a reused module f32 list.

`composite(a, img, color, alpha, over)` turns the coverage into pixels of img, an RGBA byte list for an image a.w by a.h, and clears the accumulator for the next draw. For each row from y0 up to y1: walk the row's blocks in order with a running sum that starts at 0. In a block whose mask bit is set, each cell in turn is added to the sum and set back to 0, and the pixel gets coverage equal to the smaller of 1 and the absolute value of the sum. In a block whose bit is clear every pixel gets that same coverage without reading cells, and when the absolute sum is below 1 over 512 such blocks are skipped entirely. Only columns below w are written; cells at w and w plus 1 are only cleared. A pixel with coverage c is blended with weight c times alpha toward color (0xRRGGBB). When over is false the image is opaque and blending is done in integers: w8 is the weight times 256 rounded to an int; when w8 is 256 or more the color channels are stored directly, otherwise each channel becomes old plus ((color channel minus old) times w8) shifted right by 8 (an arithmetic shift of a signed int); blending is written inline in the loops, with the color channels as ints computed once per composite; when over is true the image has straight alpha and the color is drawn over it with that alpha (result alpha is the weight plus the old alpha times one minus the weight, and each channel is the color channel times the weight plus the old channel times the old alpha times one minus the weight, divided by the result alpha, all with alpha in 0 to 1 and channels rounded to bytes). Pixels with weight below 1 over 512 are left alone. Afterwards the touched rows' mask words are all 0 and y0 and y1 are reset.

`stroke_ring(a, pts, start, count, xf, width, closed, dash, gap, caps)` strokes a line through count points of the f32 list pts starting at point index start (x at pts[2 times index], y after it), each mapped to pixels by xf, with a total width in pixels. Each segment with a length above 0.001 pixels becomes the quad p plus n, q plus n, q minus n, p minus n added with `acc_poly`, where n is the segment's direction turned a quarter (minus dy, dx), divided by its length, times half the width. When dash is 0: round joins are added with `acc_circle` of radius half the width at every point that is not an end of an open line (every point when closed), except where the line barely turns: the join is skipped when the unit directions of the segments before and after the point have a positive dot product and half the width times the absolute value of their cross product is below 0.2 pixels, and when caps is true and the line is open also at both end points; a closed ring also strokes the segment from its last point to its first. When dash is above 0 the line is cut into dashes of length dash times width separated by gaps of length gap times width, measured along the line from its start and carried across points, and each dash piece is added as its own quad with no joins and no caps. Points are mapped into a reused module f32 list, never allocated per call.

`fill_ring(a, pts, start, count, xf)` adds the closed ring of count points starting at point index start, mapped by xf, with `acc_poly` (rings with fewer than 3 points add nothing).

`bg_color(dark)` is 0x1c2026 when dark is true, else 0xf8f4f0.

`render_tile(t, wx, wy, wsize, z, size, bleed, dark, img)` draws tile t into img, an RGBA byte list it resizes to (size plus 2 times bleed) squared times 4 bytes. wx, wy and wsize give the part of the data tile being drawn in tile units (0, 0, 1 for the whole tile; a smaller square when the view zooms past the data's zoom), z is the zoom used for style rules, size is the drawn tile's width in pixels and bleed is the extra pixels drawn past each edge so neighbouring tiles overlap. It calls `style_init`, fills img with `bg_color(dark)` and alpha 255, prepares `acc_main` for the image size, and uses the Xf with ox wx, oy wy, sc size divided by wsize, and bx and by both bleed. The pixels per CSS pixel are size divided by 512. Then for every rule i of `rules` in order whose minz is at most z and whose maxz is above z: the width is `rule_width(rule, z)` times the pixels per CSS pixel and the alpha is the rule's alpha; when a line rule's width is below 1 pixel the alpha is multiplied by the width and the width becomes 1. For every feature whose mask has bit i and whose bounding box, grown by the width in tile units plus the bleed in tile units, overlaps the drawn square, each of its rings k (from the feature's r0 up to but not including r1; ring k starts at point index t.rings[2 k] and has t.rings[2 k plus 1] points) is filled with `fill_ring` (GEOM_FILL), stroked open with round caps (GEOM_LINE), or stroked closed (GEOM_EDGE), passing the rule's dash and gap. A rule that added nothing is skipped; otherwise it is composited with `rule_color(rule, dark)`, the alpha and over false.
